## 4. Terraform Port-Change Experiment

I changed the external port from 8081 to 8082 and ran:

```bash
terraform plan -var="image=bebenoo/campus-event-notifier:v1"
```

Terraform indicated that the Docker container needed to be destroyed and recreated because the external port change required resource replacement.

After applying the changes, Terraform successfully created the replacement container.

**Result:**

The application was accessible on port 8082. However, the previously created event and administrator account were lost.

This happened because the SQLite database was stored inside the container rather than in persistent storage. When Terraform replaced the container, the database data was lost.

This experiment demonstrated why persistent storage is important when deploying stateful applications.

After completing the experiment, I successfully destroyed the Terraform-managed resources using:

```bash
terraform destroy -var="image=bebenoo/campus-event-notifier:v1"
```

